---
import Layout from '@ui/layouts/Layout.astro';
import { site } from '@core/config/site';
import { getAllOffers } from '@domain/offers/offers-source';
import { categories } from '@domain/offers/categories';

// Counts come from the catalogue, so this page cannot drift into claiming a size
// the directory does not have.
const offers = await getAllOffers();
const offerCount = offers.length;
const categoryCount = categories.filter((c) => offers.some((o) => o.data.category === c.slug)).length;
---

<Layout
  title="About"
  description="Who runs Studely, how offers are chosen and scored, what the site deliberately does not do, and how it is funded."
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1>Who runs Studely</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Studely is made by one person, in Romania. It is not a company, not a team, and not
        a content farm — which is worth saying plainly, because it explains both what the
        site is good at and what it is not.
      </p>

      <h2>Why it exists</h2>
      <p>
        Student discounts are scattered across provider pages that rarely say the thing you
        actually need to know: whether you qualify, whether it is genuinely free, what
        happens when the free part runs out, and whether the deal is even still alive. The
        lists that aggregate them are mostly link dumps, ordered by nothing in particular
        and rarely updated.
      </p>
      <p>
        So Studely is small on purpose. There are <strong>{offerCount} offers</strong> across
        {categoryCount} categories right now. Each one was read, checked against the
        provider's own terms, and scored by hand before it was published. Adding a hundred
        more by scraping would take an afternoon and would make the site worse.
      </p>

      <h2>How an offer gets on the site</h2>
      <p>
        An offer is listed only if the eligibility path is clear — a school email, SheerID,
        the provider's own student programme, or similar. If I cannot work out who actually
        qualifies, it does not go up.
      </p>
      <p>
        Everything is then scored against the same fixed criteria, starting from a neutral 5
        and moving up or down for named reasons: a bundle of paid products, an instant
        eligibility check, a credit card required up front, credit that expires. Every point
        is shown on the offer's own page, so you can disagree with a specific reason instead
        of a mystery number. The full method is on <a href="/methodology">How we score</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>What Studely deliberately does not do</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>It does not claim to verify offers.</strong> There is no human
          verification log yet, so no page here says "verified" — that would be a promise
          without a record behind it. A weekly job re-fetches every link, and anything it
          cannot reach gets looked at by hand.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>It does not sell placement.</strong> No offer is here because someone paid
          for it, and payment can never move a score. If a sponsored placement ever runs, it
          will be labelled on the offer itself.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>It does not collect anything about you.</strong> Reading the directory
          needs no account, and there is no analytics of any kind — I genuinely do not know
          how many people visit. Sign-in exists only for me, to edit the catalogue.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>It does not pretend to be bigger than it is.</strong> The counts above are
          read from the database when the page is built.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How it is funded</h2>
      <p>{site.affiliateDisclosure}</p>
      <p>
        The intention is affiliate links and advertising, because a directory that costs
        nothing to read has to pay for itself somehow. When either starts earning, it will
        be stated here and on the affected offer in plain language — not buried in a
        footer. The
        <a href="/legal/disclosure">disclosure page</a> is kept in the present tense for
        exactly this reason.
      </p>

      <h2>Getting it wrong</h2>
      <p>
        Offers change without warning, and some of what is on this site will be out of date
        before I notice. If you find a dead link, a changed price, or eligibility rules that
        no longer match what we say — <a href="/contact">tell me</a> and I will fix or pull
        the listing. Always confirm the current terms on the provider's own site before
        relying on an offer.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
